/* book exam traps: Bitwise ~ is not logical !. ~4'b0011 is 1100 = 12; !4'b0011 is 0 because the operand is nonzero (true). | Reduction & returns one bit; bitwise & returns a vector. &4'b1111 is 1, while 4'b1111 & 4'b1010 is 1010. | An unsized decimal literal like 10 is 32 bits. Mixing it with a 4-bit vector can widen the result; write 4'd10 when 4 bits are intended. | Conditional ?: has very low precedence. a + b > c ? x : y groups as (a + b > c) ? x : y, not as a + (b > c ? x : y). | A wire cannot store a value assigned inside an always block. Use a reg variable for procedural storage, and a wire for continuous drive. */
